<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>false</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <WarnOn>3390;$(WarnOn)</WarnOn>
    <!-- FS0025 — an incomplete pattern match — is an ERROR here, repository
         wide, and that is a deliberate escalation rather than a strictness
         preference.

         This repository's whole vocabulary is other people's: `Transform`,
         `WindowFn`, `AggFn`, the action union, the effect sets. Every one of
         them is a closed DU owned by a producer that ships on its own rhythm,
         so the way a case arrives here is a package raise, not an edit anyone
         makes in this tree. As a warning, FS0025 is exactly the signal that
         gets scrolled past on a green build — and the cost of scrolling past it
         is a `MatchFailureException` on the first document that reaches the
         unhandled case, in a match whose own comment declares it total.
         Six such warnings stood for three tier raises before anyone read them.

         Scoped to the whole repository rather than to the files that drifted,
         because the next case will arrive in a different file and a guard that
         only covers the places already caught out is a record of the last
         failure rather than a check. Deliberately NOT `TreatWarningsAsErrors`:
         that would make an unrelated upstream deprecation a build break here,
         which is a different trade and one nobody has argued for. -->
    <WarningsAsErrors>FS0025;$(WarningsAsErrors)</WarningsAsErrors>
    <!-- 0.2.0 carries two things, both of them reasons a consumer must take a
         new slot rather than a re-pack of 0.1.0.

         First, `Fuaran.Program.Bounded` gained the channel connection and its
         out-of-band apply entry point. Additive: nothing existing changed shape,
         so no consumer stops compiling.

         Second, and the sharper of the two: the whole UI-tier floor moved from
         0.46.0 to 0.70.0, so every assembly here is COMPILED against a different
         tier. That is not a source change a consumer can read off a diff — the
         binding is in the emitted IL, against member signatures and DU case
         ordinals the tier reordered along the way. A consumer that took 0.1.0
         and a 0.70.0 tier got a `MissingMethodException` at the entry points,
         which is exactly what a version exists to prevent, so this is a new slot
         and never a re-pack. The one source-visible edge of it is the clipboard
         payload, which is a `TextSource` here now because the tier widened it.

         The producer-side rule underneath both: a change to a package's public
         contract — or, as here, to the contract it was BUILT against — advances
         the version in the SAME commit, because a folder feed keys on id+version
         and re-packing a slot in place swaps the contract under every consumer
         whose cache still serves the old bits. This line versions all four
         packages together, which is the repository's shape: a minor bump on an
         untouched package costs nothing, and a per-package line would have to be
         right four times. -->
    <!-- 0.4.0 is a NEW SLOT rather than a re-pack of 0.3.0 for the plainest of
         the reasons this comment block has recorded: v0.3.0 is TAGGED. A
         released slot is some consumer's contract, and this change costs them
         source edits, so it cannot be published over the top of one.

         What a consumer pays. The UI tier's action union widened
         `Navigate` — its route is a text SOURCE now rather than a literal, and
         it carries a browsing-context target — and gained two cases. So every
         positional match over the union in a consumer's own code stops
         compiling, and the client-effect vocabulary widened in step. The floor
         moves with it: the tier pins here are 0.78.1, so nothing built against
         an earlier tier links against these assemblies. That second half is the
         one a consumer cannot read off a source diff, and it is why the 0.2.0
         entry below argued the same case at length — the binding is in the
         emitted IL, against member signatures and case ordinals the tier moved.

         What it BUYS, stated because a version that only costs is one people
         defer. A bound route is now expressible: `Navigate` reads a binding at
         dispatch time, so "open the order the reader selected" is a document
         rather than a host closure. A route that fails to resolve navigates
         nowhere and says so, which is the tier's own rule and not this
         placement's invention. `Focus` reaches an effect arm the vocabulary has
         carried since it was cut and nothing on the wire could name.

         One thing deliberately did NOT move, and it is the reason this is a
         minor rather than something larger: a `Confirm` action is a documented
         no-op on the bounded path, diagnosed like every other. The round trip
         it needs — ask, answer, re-validate, then dispatch the continuation
         through its own gate — is a placement capability, not a raise, and
         claiming it here would put a version number on work nobody has done.

         This line versions all four packages together, which is the
         repository's shape: a minor bump on an untouched package costs nothing,
         and a per-package line would have to be right four times. -->
    <!-- 0.5.0 — Phase 1689. A NEW SLOT for the same plainest reason: v0.4.0 is
         TAGGED, and this change moves the specification's FORMAT VERSION, which
         is the least re-packable kind of change there is.

         The program wire specification is at format version 2. §5.2's
         client-effect vocabulary was closed at six arms while a conformant
         emitter already shipped two more, so a rendering surface was receiving
         documents the text declared ill-formed; §11.1 rules that widening a
         closed vocabulary is BREAKING and that the fifth artefact of such a
         change-set is the codec of every host certifying against the corpus.
         `decodeClientEffect` is one of those codecs, and it now covers eight
         arms: `Print`, which carries no members and refuses any (an accepted
         member would leave an emitter believing it had constrained a printing
         it had not), and `Confirm`, whose `prompt` and `token` are both
         required and whose continuations are deliberately absent from the wire.

         What a consumer pays, and it is less than 0.4.0 cost. Nothing in this
         repository's own surface changed shape: the arms come from the UI tier's
         `ClientEffect`, which has carried all eight since 0.78.0, so the pins
         are untouched and no positional match over anything here breaks. What
         moved is which DOCUMENTS this codec accepts, and it moved in one
         direction only — a version-1 document decodes exactly as it did, pinned
         by a test that walks all six original arms and both of `Navigate`'s
         canonical spellings, and only a version-2 document is newly admitted. A
         consumer whose own reader is still at version 1 will refuse what this
         one now takes, which is what the version number is for.

         And the thing that deliberately did NOT move, exactly as the 0.4.0 entry
         above said of it: a `Confirm` action is still a documented no-op on the
         bounded path. Its round trip — ask, answer, re-validate, dispatch the
         continuation through its own gate — is a placement capability, and
         declaring the arm on the wire does not perform it. What changed is that
         the instruction is now expressible; what performs it is still nobody
         here. -->
    <!-- Phase 1744 RIDES 0.5.0 rather than advancing it. 0.5.0 is untagged (the
         newest tag is v0.4.0) and pinned by no public-path consumer, so it is a
         draft slot, and the signed effect envelope is additive: a new
         `SignedEnvelope` module beside `Demanded`, two functions on
         `ServerDemanded`, and no change to any existing shape or wire. A draft
         slot takes an additive change without moving; only a change of a higher
         class than the slot already carries advances it. -->
    <!-- Phase 1742 RIDES 0.5.0 too, on the same reading. The denial detector is
         a new module (`DenialDetector`) beside the ones already shipping, plus
         one function on `ServerDemanded` that stops being private so the
         detector and the argument-policy join read the SAME capability set
         rather than two enumerations of it. Nothing existing changed shape, no
         wire moved, and no dependency was added — the detector consumes the
         denial sink and the control vocabulary this package already carries. An
         additive change against an untagged, unpinned draft slot, so the number
         stays where it is. -->
    <!-- 0.6.0 — Phase 1896. ADVANCES rather than rides: v0.5.0 is TAGGED, and
         this change is breaking — the three core packages become
         domain-generic (their public types gain type parameters) and the
         UI tier leaves their dependency graph. What broke, what a consumer
         does about it, and why the core and its UI adapter release together
         are recorded in STABILITY.md, which holds this slot as a DRAFT:
         untagged and unreleased until the adapter ships beside it. -->
    <!-- 0.7.0 — Phase 1967. ADVANCES rather than rides: v0.6.0 is TAGGED, and
         this change is breaking — the action view gains a fifth shape (the
         halting guard `Require`), the fold's outcome gains a `Halted` member,
         the op witness gains `Reach`, the demanded document moves to version 5,
         the argument policy takes the op witness, and the handler's `run`
         gains a `runWith` beside it that takes how a placement performs an op.
         Every one of those is a record gaining a member or a function gaining
         a parameter, which is a source edit for every consumer (FS0764 on each
         full-literal witness construction). What a consumer pays and why each
         change is there — the second domain's four findings, recorded in
         DECISIONS.md D19 — is in STABILITY.md, which holds this slot as a
         DRAFT until it is tagged. -->
    <!-- 0.8.0 — Phase 1905. ADVANCES rather than rides: v0.7.1 is TAGGED,
         and this change is breaking by this repository's own classification —
         `ServerEffectRegistry` gains `QueryEvaluator` and `PerformerFacets`
         gains `QueryEvaluator`, a record gaining a member (FS0764 on a
         full-literal construction). Every other change is additive: the query
         evaluator seam, its law family, and the registration and declaration
         functions beside it (DECISIONS.md D34). STABILITY.md holds the slot as
         a DRAFT until it is tagged. -->
    <!-- 0.9.0 — Phase 2197. ADVANCES rather than rides: v0.8.0 is TAGGED, and
         this change is breaking — an op performer returns a typed `OpReceipt`
         rather than a `JVal`, `OpPrefix.Receipts` and `OpContract` take it,
         and `HandlerOutcome` and `HandlerTally` gain `Receipts` (FS0764 on a
         full literal). DECISIONS.md D43 records the decision; STABILITY.md
         holds the slot as a DRAFT until it is tagged. -->
    <!-- 0.10.0 — the substrate raise to Fuaran.Core 0.38.1 and Fuaran.Compute.DataFrame
         0.40.0. ADVANCES rather than rides: v0.9.0 is TAGGED, and the contract every
         package is BUILT against moved across Core 0.37.0's breaking release — a
         schema entry is a `Field`, a `Column` carries typed vectors and is no longer
         comparable. A consumer of 0.9.0 on Core 0.37.0 or later reaches members its
         binaries no longer find. No member of this repository's own surface changes.
         STABILITY.md holds the slot as a DRAFT until it is tagged. -->
    <!-- 0.10.1 — Phase 2220. ADVANCES rather than rides: v0.10.0 is TAGGED.
         ADDITIVE: the derived view (`DerivedView`, `Derivations`,
         `Durable.runDeriving`) is new; no existing type, member or case moves
         (DECISIONS.md D47). The relation primitives 2220 also added were deleted
         before tag by Phase 2382 (D49): they live in Fuaran.Core.Relation.
         0.10.2: the release of the above. 0.10.1 was packed locally while it still
         carried the relation primitives, so it names a different contract and is
         never released (RM-VERSION-SURFACE-MOVED). -->
    <!-- 0.11.0 — Phase 2228, the substrate raise to Fuaran.Core 0.40.2 and
         Fuaran.Compute.DataFrame 0.41.0. ADVANCES rather than rides: 0.10.2 is
         published, and the contract every package is BUILT against moved across
         Core 0.39.0's breaking release — `Fuaran.Core.Canon` and
         `Json.parseDetailed` are gone (Core D188, Phase 519). A consumer of
         0.10.2 on Core 0.39.0 or later reaches members its binaries no longer
         find. No member of this repository's own surface changes, and no byte of
         the program wire moves. Released as `v0.11.0`. -->
    <Version>0.11.0</Version>
    <!-- This repository's restore path is PUBLIC: it publishes to nuget.org, so
         a consumer of the published packages reaches the registry and nothing
         else. Undeclared would read as `local-feed`, under which a pin only this
         machine's folder feed can serve looks correct. `tools/check-pins.ps1`
         already enforces the same rule before every build; this declares it to
         the estate sweep as well, so a raise toward a version the producer has
         cut but not released is refused rather than reported afterwards. -->
    <FuaranRestorePath>public</FuaranRestorePath>
    <Authors>Fuaran</Authors>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>Apache-2.0</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <!-- Phase 2015 - the repository's home since 2026-10-09 (transferred and renamed; the package ids
         are unchanged). -->
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/Fuaran-Core/fuaran-program-dotnet</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/Fuaran-Core/fuaran-program-dotnet</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
